#slide()[
  #text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Subtracting Fractions]
  #v(40pt)

  #only("1-")[🍕 Pizza Party Dilemma]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("2-")[- Sarah ate 3/8 of a pizza]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("3-")[- Tom ate 1/8 of the same pizza]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("4-")[- How much pizza is left? 🤔]

  #only("1")[
    #voiceover("Welcome to our lesson on subtracting fractions! Imagine you're at a pizza party.")
  ]
  #only("2")[
    #voiceover("Sarah has eaten three-eighths of a pizza.")
  ]
  #only("3")[
    #voiceover("Tom has eaten one-eighth of the same pizza.")
  ]
  #only("4")[
    #voiceover("Now, we need to figure out how much pizza is left. This is where subtracting fractions comes in handy!")
  ]
]


#slide()[
  #text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Same Denominator]
  #v(40pt)

  #only("1-")[- Subtract numerators, keep denominator]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("2-")[$ 3/8 - 1/8 = (3-1)/8 = 2/8 $]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("3-")[🍕 Pizza left: $ 1 - (3/8 + 1/8) = 1 - 4/8 = 4/8 $]

  #only("1")[
    #voiceover("When subtracting fractions with the same denominator, it's simple: we just subtract the numerators and keep the same denominator.")
  ]
  #only("2")[
    #voiceover("In our pizza example, we have three-eighths minus one-eighth. That's three minus one, over eight, which equals two-eighths.")
  ]
  #only("3")[
    #voiceover("To find out how much pizza is left, we subtract the total eaten from the whole pizza. One minus four-eighths equals four-eighths. So half of the pizza is left!")
  ]
]


#slide()[
  #text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Different Denominators]
  #v(40pt)

  #only("1-")[- Find common denominator first]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("2-")[- Example: $ 1/2 - 1/3 $]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("3-")[- Common denominator: 6]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("4-")[$ 1/2 = 3/6 $, $ 1/3 = 2/6 $]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("5-")[$ 3/6 - 2/6 = 1/6 $]

  #only("1")[
    #voiceover("When subtracting fractions with different denominators, we need to find a common denominator first.")
  ]
  #only("2")[
    #voiceover("Let's look at a new example: one-half minus one-third.")
  ]
  #only("3")[
    #voiceover("The common denominator for halves and thirds is six.")
  ]
  #only("4")[
    #voiceover("We convert one-half to three-sixths, and one-third to two-sixths.")
  ]
  #only("5")[
    #voiceover("Now we can subtract: three-sixths minus two-sixths equals one-sixth.")
  ]
]


#slide()[
  #text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Summary]
  #v(40pt)

  #only("1-")[- Same denominator: subtract numerators only]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("2-")[- Different denominators: find common denominator first]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("3-")[- Practice makes perfect! 🏆]

  #only("1")[
    #voiceover("To summarize, when subtracting fractions with the same denominator, we simply subtract the numerators and keep the same denominator.")
  ]
  #only("2")[
    #voiceover("When dealing with different denominators, we first need to find a common denominator before subtracting.")
  ]
  #only("3")[
    #voiceover("Remember, practice is key to mastering fraction subtraction. Keep working on it, and you'll be a fraction expert in no time!")
  ]
]